" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-chevron-down" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg></summary><div><a href="/nyc/brooklyn/">Brooklyn</a><a href="/nyc/manhattan/">Manhattan</a><a href="/nyc/brooklyn/#williamsburg">Williamsburg &amp; DUMBO</a><a href="/nyc/manhattan/#midtown">Midtown &amp; Chelsea</a></div></details><a href="/nyc/projects/">Projects</a><a href="/nyc/about/">About</a><a href="/nyc/resources/">Resources</a></nav><a class="btn primary header-quote" href="/nyc/contact/">Request a quote <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="17" height="17" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 7h10v10"></path><path d="M7 17 17 7"></path></svg></a><details class="export-mobile"><summary>Menu</summary><nav><a href="/nyc/">Home</a><a href="/nyc/products/">Products</a><a href="/nyc/interior-signs/">Interior Signs</a><a href="/nyc/exterior-signs/">Exterior Signs</a><a href="/nyc/vehicle-wraps/">Vehicle Wraps</a><a href="/nyc/custom-signs/">Custom Signs</a><a href="/nyc/trade-show-displays/">Trade Show Displays</a><a href="/nyc/retractable-banners/">Retractable Banners</a><a href="/nyc/brooklyn/">Brooklyn</a><a href="/nyc/manhattan/">Manhattan</a><a href="/nyc/projects/">Projects</a><a href="/nyc/gallery/">Gallery</a><a href="/nyc/resources/">Resources</a><a href="/nyc/contact/">Contact</a></nav></details></div></header><div class="product-ribbon"><div class="wrap"><a href="/nyc/interior-signs/">Interior signs</a><a href="/nyc/exterior-signs/">Exterior signs</a><a href="/nyc/vehicle-wraps/">Vehicle wraps</a><a href="/nyc/custom-signs/">Custom signs</a><a href="/nyc/trade-show-displays/">Trade show displays</a><a href="/nyc/retractable-banners/">Retractable banners</a></div></div><main><section class="wrap product-hero"><div><p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/nyc/">New York</a> / Wayfinding signs for a complete route</p><p class="eyebrow">VISION GRAPHICS · NEW YORK</p><h1>Wayfinding signs for a complete route</h1><p class="lead">Wayfinding brings directories, directions and destination signs into one system. The aim is to help people build a useful picture of the place rather than follow a disconnected series of arrows. Vision Graphics can help New York offices and managed properties plan a coordinated approach from entry to arrival. A clear project begins with the routes, room names and visitor needs, then uses consistent visual rules so each sign confirms the next step without competing with the building’s other information and branding.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn primary" href="#request-quote">Discuss your project <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="17" height="17" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 7h10v10"></path><path d="M7 17 17 7"></path></svg></a><a class="text-link" href="#options">Explore options <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-right" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12h14"></path><path d="m12 5 7 7-7 7"></path></svg></a></div></div><figure class="project-image product-photo"><img src="/nyc/assets/mcs-new-service-custom-signs-960.webp" srcset="/nyc/assets/mcs-new-service-custom-signs-480.webp 480w, /nyc/assets/mcs-new-service-custom-signs-960.webp 960w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 50vw" alt="Signage example related to Wayfinding signs for a complete route" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Illustrative sign example · Michigan Custom Signs source</figcaption></figure></section><section class="wrap section article-layout"><article><section><h2>Build the information system first</h2><p>Agree on destination names and a hierarchy for building, floor, department and room information. A visitor should not have to infer that two different labels refer to the same place. Use the floor plan to identify where orientation, direction and confirmation are needed.</p></section><section><h2>Test the route before production</h2><p>Review proposed locations by walking through the space and checking sightlines. Confirm arrow direction, obstructions and the relationship to doors and turns. Keep an approved sign schedule so the system remains coherent when spaces or occupants change.</p></section><section><h2>Choose a system that can handle change</h2><p>Before choosing finishes, identify what changes regularly: tenant names, departments, meeting-room names or floor layouts. A fixed panel may suit a stable destination, while an updateable component may make future changes easier. Separate the core route information from branding and temporary notices so visitors can still find the next decision clearly.</p></section><section><h2>What affects the quote and site work</h2><p>The number of routes, buildings or floors, sign locations, unique messages, materials and mounting methods affect the scope. Design effort also depends on whether an approved schedule already exists or the information system needs to be organized. Installation access, removal of old signs, work-hour restrictions and future replacement needs should be included in the brief.</p></section><section><h2>Send a route-based project brief</h2><p>Provide floor plans, the approved list of destinations and photographs of entrances, corridors and existing signs. Identify visitor entry points, common wrong turns and locations where people ask for directions. Add quantities if known, brand guidance, a target date and the property contact. Confirm who reviews accessibility requirements, approves placement and maintains the sign schedule after installation.</p></section><section id="options"><p class="eyebrow">MATERIALS &amp; APPROACHES</p><h2>Find the right fit.</h2><div class="options-grid"><div><h3>Orientation directories</h3><p>Show destinations at entrances, reception points or floor arrivals. Organize information using names visitors will also see along the route.</p></div><div><h3>Directional sign families</h3><p>Guide decisions with consistent names, arrows and a clear hierarchy. Review each arrow in the context of its actual proposed location.</p></div><div><h3>Destination identification</h3><p>Confirm arrival at a suite, room or department. Coordinate with the approved room identification and accessible-sign specifications.</p></div><div><h3>Updateable information systems</h3><p>Explore changeable names or components where tenant and room information turns over. Confirm how replacements will be ordered and installed.</p></div></div></section><section><h2>Where it can work</h2><ul class="check-list"><li><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-check" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5"></path></svg>Office relocations</li><li><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-check" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5"></path></svg>Multi-tenant properties</li><li><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-check" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5"></path></svg>Visitor-facing facilities</li><li><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-check" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5"></path></svg>Shared reception and meeting spaces</li><li><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-check" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5"></path></svg>Phased building and tenant refreshes</li></ul></section><section class="faqs"><h2>A few useful answers.</h2><details><summary>What is the difference from a directional sign?<span>+</span></summary><p>A directional sign is one component. Wayfinding considers the complete route and the relationship between all information points.</p></details><details><summary>Can an existing system be improved in stages?<span>+</span></summary><p>An audit can identify naming conflicts and missing decision points, then guide a phased update.</p></details><details><summary>Do we need a floor plan before requesting a quote?<span>+</span></summary><p>A current floor plan makes the brief much more useful, even if the sign locations are not finalized. Add a destination list and photos of decision points. If a plan is unavailable, explain what records and site access can be provided so the scope can be reviewed.</p></details><details><summary>Can new signs work alongside our current system?<span>+</span></summary><p>They can be planned to do so. Share the existing naming rules, sign types and any approved specification. Review the full visitor route to avoid inconsistent names, competing arrows or missing confirmation points between old and new areas.</p></details></section></article><aside class="product-aside"><p class="eyebrow">LET’S START WITH THE DETAILS</p><h3>Have a space in mind?</h3><p>A photo, a few measurements and your project ZIP help us ask the right questions.</p><a href="/nyc/contact/?photo=1" class="btn dark"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="17" height="17" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-camera" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13.997 4a2 2 0 0 1 1.76 1.05l.486.9A2 2 0 0 0 18.003 7H20a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v9a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V9a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h1.997a2 2 0 0 0 1.759-1.048l.489-.904A2 2 0 0 1 10.004 4z"></path><circle cx="12" cy="13" r="3"></circle></svg> Send a project photo</a><hr><h3>Related possibilities</h3><a href="/nyc/directional-signs/">Directional signs<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="15" height="15" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 7h10v10"></path><path d="M7 17 17 7"></path></svg></a><a href="/nyc/interior-directory-signs/">Interior directory signs<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="15" height="15" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 7h10v10"></path><path d="M7 17 17 7"></path></svg></a><a href="/nyc/office-signs/">Office signs<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="15" height="15" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 7h10v10"></path><path d="M7 17 17 7"></path></svg></a><a href="/nyc/ada-signs/">ADA signs<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="15" height="15" viewbox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="lucide lucide-arrow-up-right" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 7h10v10"></path><path d="M7 17 17 7"></path></svg></a><a href="/nyc/offices-properties/">Offices &amp; properties<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg